#: source/advanced-topics/cores-vs-jobs.md:1
msgid "Tuning Cores and Jobs"
msgstr ""

#: source/advanced-topics/cores-vs-jobs.md:3
msgid ""
"Nix has two relevant settings with regards to how your CPU cores will be "
"utilized: `cores` and `max-jobs`. This chapter will talk about what they "
"are, how they interact, and their configuration trade-offs."
msgstr ""

#: source/advanced-topics/cores-vs-jobs.md:7
msgid ""
"`max-jobs`  \n"
"Dictates how many separate derivations will be built at the same time. If "
"you set this to zero, the local machine will do no builds.  Nix will still "
"substitute from binary caches, and build remotely if remote builders are "
"configured."
msgstr ""

#: source/advanced-topics/cores-vs-jobs.md:13
msgid ""
"`cores`  \n"
"Suggests how many cores each derivation should use. Similar to `make -j`."
msgstr ""

#: source/advanced-topics/cores-vs-jobs.md:17
msgid ""
"The `cores` setting determines the value of `NIX_BUILD_CORES`. "
"`NIX_BUILD_CORES` is equal to `cores`, unless `cores` equals `0`, in which "
"case `NIX_BUILD_CORES` will be the total number of cores in the system."
msgstr ""

#: source/advanced-topics/cores-vs-jobs.md:22
msgid ""
"The maximum number of consumed cores is a simple multiplication, `max-jobs` "
"\\* `NIX_BUILD_CORES`."
msgstr ""

#: source/advanced-topics/cores-vs-jobs.md:25
msgid ""
"The balance on how to set these two independent variables depends upon each "
"builder's workload and hardware. Here are a few example scenarios on a "
"machine with 24 cores:"
msgstr ""

#: source/advanced-topics/cores-vs-jobs.md:29
msgid "`max-jobs`"
msgstr "`max-jobs`"

#: source/advanced-topics/cores-vs-jobs.md:29
msgid "`cores`"
msgstr "`cores`"

#: source/advanced-topics/cores-vs-jobs.md:29
msgid "`NIX_BUILD_CORES`"
msgstr ""

#: source/advanced-topics/cores-vs-jobs.md:29
msgid "Maximum Processes"
msgstr ""

#: source/advanced-topics/cores-vs-jobs.md:29
msgid "Result"
msgstr ""

#: source/advanced-topics/cores-vs-jobs.md:31
#: source/advanced-topics/cores-vs-jobs.md:34
msgid "1"
msgstr ""

#: source/advanced-topics/cores-vs-jobs.md:31
#: source/advanced-topics/cores-vs-jobs.md:32
#: source/advanced-topics/cores-vs-jobs.md:34
#: source/advanced-topics/cores-vs-jobs.md:35
msgid "24"
msgstr "24"

#: source/advanced-topics/cores-vs-jobs.md:31
msgid ""
"One derivation will be built at a time, each one can use 24 cores. Undersold "
"if a job can’t use 24 cores."
msgstr ""

#: source/advanced-topics/cores-vs-jobs.md:32
msgid "4"
msgstr ""

#: source/advanced-topics/cores-vs-jobs.md:32
#: source/advanced-topics/cores-vs-jobs.md:33
msgid "6"
msgstr ""

#: source/advanced-topics/cores-vs-jobs.md:32
msgid "Four derivations will be built at once, each given access to six cores."
msgstr ""

#: source/advanced-topics/cores-vs-jobs.md:33
msgid "12"
msgstr ""

#: source/advanced-topics/cores-vs-jobs.md:33
msgid "72"
msgstr ""

#: source/advanced-topics/cores-vs-jobs.md:33
msgid ""
"12 derivations will be built at once, each given access to six cores. This "
"configuration is over-sold. If all 12 derivations being built simultaneously "
"try to use all six cores, the machine's performance will be degraded due to "
"extensive context switching between the 12 builds."
msgstr ""

#: source/advanced-topics/cores-vs-jobs.md:34
msgid ""
"24 derivations can build at the same time, each using a single core. Never "
"oversold, but derivations which require many cores will be very slow to "
"compile."
msgstr ""

#: source/advanced-topics/cores-vs-jobs.md:35
msgid "0"
msgstr ""

#: source/advanced-topics/cores-vs-jobs.md:35
msgid "576"
msgstr ""

#: source/advanced-topics/cores-vs-jobs.md:35
msgid ""
"24 derivations can build at the same time, each using all the available "
"cores of the machine. Very likely to be oversold, and very likely to suffer "
"context switches."
msgstr ""

#: source/advanced-topics/cores-vs-jobs.md:37
msgid ""
"It is up to the derivations' build script to respect host's requested cores-"
"per-build by following the value of the `NIX_BUILD_CORES` environment "
"variable."
msgstr ""
